## Why This Is Asked
This tests reasoning with Kleene 3-valued logic in interdependent constraints, and the ability to design a propagation/backtracking solver. It also requires concrete verification on a small example.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics for ∧, ∨, ¬, →, ↔
- Constraint propagation and backtracking with U values
- Handling cycles and termination guarantees

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Kleene 3-valued helpers
const val = {T:2, U:1, F:0};
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function land(a,b){
  const table = {T:{T:'T',U:'U',F:'F'}, U:{T:'U',U:'U',F:'F'}, F:{T:'F',U:'F',F:'F'}};
  return table[a][b];
}
function lor(a,b){
  const table = {T:{T:'T',U:'T',F:'T'}, U:{T:'T',U:'U',F:'U'}, F:{T:'T',U:'U',F:'F'}};
  return table[a][b];
}
function imp(a,b){ return lor(not(a), b); }
function equiv(a,b){ return land(imp(a,b), imp(b,a)); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to detect unsat when no U is possible?
- How would you scale to 10+ variables with more operators and cycles?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a new, well-defined operator (Maj) within Kleene-like three-valued logic and requires concrete CSP reasoning with backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic semantics
- a custom 3-ary Maj operator (majority with T/F bias)
- constraint propagation and backtracking in non-binary domains

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function maj(a,b,c){
  const t=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='T').length;
  const f=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='F').length;
  if(t>=2) return 'T';
  if(f>=2) return 'F';
  return 'U';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend Maj to 4 inputs while preserving monotonicity?
- What is the impact of introducing additional unknowns on backtracking complexity?
